# kurzgesagt fix log

## epoch 0 (2026-11-20): created from a reference reel
Made by Claude from references/kurzgesagt.mp4: a 4.0 s, 1080 x 1920, 30 fps, silent single-scene clip rendered from the /kurzgesagt skill of github.com/artemnovitckii/claude-animation-skills (MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Artem Novitckii). Named after the creator style at the user's request; it is not affiliated with Kurzgesagt and copies none of their characters or artwork. Analysis: reference-lab/runs/kurzgesagt/analysis-claude (survey and review answered by Claude), crops in reference-lab/runs/kurzgesagt/crops, committed copy in reference-lab/refs/kurzgesagt. rea-video found 1 shot (code render), no cuts, no audio; its subtitle track is the 3 caption swaps.

Taken from the video (by eye and measured):
- Look: a deep violet-navy ground (L 0.17 to 0.22, C 0.08 to 0.09, hue about 280) with a purple haze in the middle and a plum tint at the bottom; violet and pale lavender star dots plus four-point sparkles; flat bodies with no outlines, a lit crescent on the right and a big dark night side on the left, a soft halo of their own colour; bright rounded pill tags in each object's own hue with white text; one hot-pink answer chip with a yellow check disc, tilted; a dotted violet measuring line that turns mint at the payoff; one white sentence-case caption low in the frame. Overlay colours measured with tools/oklch_colours.py on full-resolution crops: pink L 0.65 C 0.23 hue 12, violet L 0.65 C 0.18 hue 286, mint L 0.83 to 0.87 C 0.18 to 0.20 hue 155 to 160, yellow L 0.85 C 0.16 hue 89. Background brightness spread 9.2 (validator needs 6), centre only 1.4x the corners: the template asks for a stronger haze (about 2x, the _base rule).
- Structure: a two-part question, objects arriving one at a time to scale on a measured line, the answer stamped at a visual hit (pulse, line turns mint, chip pops). Turned into topic-free beats for a full 7 to 8 shot narrated reel: hook (question), yardstick (something known, with its number), step (2 or 3, each to scale beside the previous), turn ("But ..."), answer (the column to scale and the check tag, payoff_shot), close (the subject big, like the hook).

Taken from the skill's source (skills/kurzgesagt/template.html and SKILL.md), far more exact than the video:
- Body drawing, translated from SVG into kit.body: halo (base colour circle 1.12 r at 50% with an 18 px gaussian blur, so a radial gradient from 0.9 r to 1.4 r); 7 bands at fixed offsets and heights (yy = y - r + (k + 0.5) 2r / 7, height r (0.10 + (k % 3) 0.05)) or 5 seeded blotches; 3 patch ellipses at fixed offsets; the night side as an ellipse at (x - 0.55 r, y), radii 1.02 r and 1.25 r, in the ground hue at 78%; a soft white highlight at (x + 0.35 r, y - 0.38 r), radius 0.22 r at 16% (a radial gradient instead of a blur filter); rings as an ellipse 2.15 r by 0.42 r rotated -14 degrees, 0.2 r wide, back half before the body and front (lower) half after it.
- Stars: 170 seeded dots (85% radius 1 to 3.2, 60% violet), every 4th breathing in opacity; 14 four-point sparkles from the exact quadratic-curve path; a slow 6 px/s drift (the source drifts the scene 24 px over 4 s).
- Tags: pill radius about 0.27 of its height, padding about 0.6 of the font size; tags sit 26 px beyond their thing's edge, alternating sides; things under 30 px share one tag. Answer chip: pink pill, yellow disc with a dark check (drawn as a path), rotation -8 to -3 degrees while it pops.
- Timing and easing: things slide in from 760 px off with a 40 degree rotation unwinding, 0.34 s, back.out(1.25); one every 0.29 s (0.3 s in the template); tags pop 0.2 s after landing, 0.22 s, back.out(2.2); the payoff pulse scale 1.035 over 0.12 s, then back over 0.45 s with back.out(2); the chip 0.32 s with back.out(2.6). The back() formula with a strength parameter is written into the template because h.easeBack has a fixed overshoot (1.7).
- Colours: the source's hex values turned into OKLCH relations by role around the ground hue H: night side L 0.25 C 0.11 hue H; line violet L 0.67 C 0.19 hue H + 5; pink L 0.67 C 0.23 hue H + 90; yellow L 0.86 C 0.16 hue H + 165; mint L 0.85 C 0.18 hue H - 125; per-object tag colour = the real colour made vivid (L 0.58 to 0.76, C 0.12 to 0.22), body base = tag mixed 25 to 50% toward white, details = the tag colour (checked on three of the source's objects: base lightness within about 0.04).
- Kept out on purpose: the topic, its object list, its words, numbers and distances, the chip's wording.

Checked before handing over: a reference kit written exactly from the template's look section (scratchpad kurz_kit.js) passes the engine's validateKit (brightness spread, motion) for both backgrounds, and two test frames rendered in the engine stage (a two-thing stage and a column with the answer chip) look like the reference. That test showed column tags colliding when a shared small-thing tag and a normal tag land on the same side, so the column's tag rule now places the shared tag on the left and moves any tag within 80 px of another to the other side.

Engine limits met (not changed; engine files belong to the main session):
- Captions: the reference shows one unboxed white sentence-case line at y about 1435 with a soft shadow. The engine draws its own narrator caption box at y about 1600 (a dark box with a palette.accent stripe); templates cannot change its look. palette.accent is the line violet so the stripe matches. The only other mode, `captions: words` (1 or 2 lowercase words popping at 96 px with a thick outline), is further from this calm style, so it is not used.
- One continuous take: the engine adds a horizontal smear at every cut and a 3% push per shot. The template asks for continuity instead (a thing that stays starts where it ended).
- Silent reference: no voice, music tempo or effects to measure. The narrator (george, calm and a little wry), the music (Uplifting, Mystical, Bright, 90 to 110 bpm) and the 2 effects follow the kind and _base, not a measurement.
- Font: the reference uses Poppins, which is not installed. kit.font asks for "Century Gothic" (installed here, C:/Windows/Fonts/GOTHIC*.TTF, a similar round geometric sans), falling back to Segoe UI. The plan's style.font field only lists six fonts, so the plan writes "Segoe UI" and the look section tells the kit to use the stack.
- Size check: every shot must place an asset at least 380 px wide, so the biggest thing in every shot needs R 130 or more (a box is 2.94 R). The template states it, and every named thing is an asset (the subject, plus "bodies" with a "which" param), so to-scale shots and the column pass.
- Palette: plan.txt hints at up to 10 colours. The template needs 8 style colours plus one tag colour per thing, so it allows 14. The engine does not enforce the count.
- No `hero width` / `hero centre y` checks: the subject can be small in the column (the answer shot), and those checks would reject a correct to-scale column.

epoch 1: sun-kz-e1 ("how big is the Sun", 46.7 s, 7 shots, MiniMax $0.133, reel total $0.216 with voice; grade: rendering 0.04, colour 0.03, rhythm 0.22)
- Right: the look (violet-navy star field, haloed flat bodies with night side and lit crescent, coloured name tags, dotted measuring lines with number tags, pink check chip), hook and close mirror each other. Voice george pitch spread 4.6 st PASS, music fit PASS, voice over music 11.5 dB.
- Flaw: the Sun was drawn as a crescent with a night side (reads as a moon) -> fix: things that shine are lit all over (kit.body o.star: hot core, 1.6 r halo, no night side).
- Flaw: not to scale: the Sun 3x Earth (really 109x), Betelgeuse about the Sun's size (really hundreds of times wider) -> fix: TRUE SCALE rule in the step beat: exact radius ratios, and above 6x the big one is drawn huge and cropped with the small one a disc beside its edge.
- Flaw: "109 Earths" said in 4 lines; the hook answered its own question; the close repeated the turn -> fix: hook line is only the question; each fact and number in ONE line only.
- Flaw: the answer shot showed only the dotted line for its first half, then tags piled up over each other -> fix: the column fills from the line's first word (thing i at start + 0.2 + i * 0.45), TP when the last lands.
- Flaw: the answer chip appeared from shot 3 on -> fix: chip only in the answer and close shots. Shot 3 rejected 3 times for a solid fill over 60% -> fix: glows and haze are radial gradients in a circle, never fillRect.

epoch 2: betel-kz-e2 ("how big is Betelgeuse", held-out brief; 30.1 s, 7 shots, MiniMax $0.128, reel total $0.128 with the Kokoro voice bm_george: ElevenLabs out of credits; grade: rendering 0.04, colour 0.09, rhythm 0.2)
- Fixed by epoch 1: stars lit all over (the Sun a full warm disc with a hot core, Betelgeuse a full red disc, no crescent); the hook only asks ("How large is Betelgeuse?"); the answer chip only in the answer and close shots; planets shown as small discs swallowed inside Betelgeuse's edge.
- Flaw: still not to scale: Betelgeuse at a normal R about 130 with the Sun a 6 px dot (about 20x, really about 700x), the two name tags on top of each other -> fix: a concrete recipe for ratios above 6 (the big body as a curved glowing horizon, centre at y 1250 + 2200, the small one on it; above 300 the small one a 7 px dot and the line says how many fit across).
- Flaw: "seven hundred times wider" said in the step and again in the answer -> fix: when a step said the number, the answer gives the same truth as a new vivid comparison.
- Flaw: the same red disc alone in 5 of 7 shots -> fix: the picture changes every shot; one thing alone only in the hook and the close.
- Shots 3 and 6 rejected 6 times for a solid fill over 69% and 378% of the frame (shipped anyway); the radial-gradient rule did not stop it -> watch on e3; if it repeats, make it a kit helper.
- Length 30 s against 40: Kokoro bm_george speaks 2.39 w/s and the plan kept short lines; voice pitch spread 3.29 st (flat).
